;; VERIFY-r-nodes.edn -- the node-by-node verification matrix of worklist row :V7.
;;
;; Commissioning ruling: Joe, 2026-09-06 -- "Go node by node and verify the R
;; nodes actually have what we think they should have inside them ... I'd be
;; happy to approve RUN4 if we know that there aren't massive holes"
;; (worklist.edn :V7 :statement). This file is the answer RUN4 approval reads.
;;
;; APPEND-ONLY, ONE NODE PER SLICE, in aif-equations dependency order. A slice
;; adds one map to :rows and one map to :slices; it does not edit an earlier
;; row. If a later slice contradicts an earlier one, it appends a row carrying
;; :supersedes with the earlier row's [:node :slice] pair, so the contradiction
;; is readable rather than overwritten.
;;
;; EVERY CLAIM CARRIES A POINTER -- a file:line, a run-record path with a
;; sha256, or the literal :not-found with the search that established the
;; absence. This file is scanned by p4ng/empirics-futon/pointer_check.bb (it is
;; named in that script's extra-paths), so a pointer written here that does not
;; resolve fails the gate.
;;
;; NO RULINGS. Nothing here is written to aif-equations.edn :choices or to
;; control-map-edges.edn :decisions. A :hole verdict records what the code and
;; the registry say about each other; whether a hole should be filled, and how,
;; is Joe's.
{:schema :wm/verify-r-nodes-v1
 :row :V7
 :epic "EPIC-run-era.md"

 :verdict-vocabulary
 {:verified "Every equation the registry gives the node is implemented, the implementation was run, an independent reference reproduced it, and the node's known candidate holes were cleared with evidence."
  :verified-with-stated-limits "As above, with named limits that do not change what is inside the node -- e.g. a check that ran on declared carriers rather than on a live field."
  :hole "Something the registry says the node has, the node does not have; or the node has something the registry does not say it has. :type names which."}

 :hole-types
 {:declared-grain-not-implemented "The registry's equations are transcribed and correct, but no shipped call path evaluates them at the grain the registry declares; what ships computes a different object under the same name."
  :uninhabited-carrier "An argument the equation requires has no inhabitant, in Lean or at runtime, other than a fixture."
  :key-collision "Two different quantities are persisted under one key, so a reader of the trace cannot tell which one a record carries."
  :pointer-drift "A registry :code or :basis pointer resolves to a line that no longer contains what the field claims."
  :cross-node-grain-mix "Two terms of one equation are evaluated at different values of a shared parameter inside a single call."
  :declared-source-not-read "An input the node's declared scope names is present in the node's own argument and is never read by it -- so the gap is at the node's binding form, not upstream of it."
  :retired-quantity-still-required-elsewhere "A quantity the equations registry retired by ruling is still required, in its retired form, by a document the registry does not gate. The node satisfies the registry and fails that document, and nothing in either records the difference. Added by slice 3; the vocabulary is extended rather than a row rewritten."
  :graded-satisfied-on-an-unmet-criterion "A contract document records the node's operational criterion as SATISFIED, and neither clause of that criterion's own check holds: one is unmet by the carrier the node ships, and the other was graded at a date preceding the code that could satisfy it. The node meets its equations-registry row; it does not meet the document that grades it, and the document carries no record of the difference. Added by slice 4; the vocabulary is extended rather than a row rewritten."
  :graded-on-a-superseded-implementation "A contract document grades the node satisfied on a description of an implementation the node no longer has. The grade is not merely optimistic: it is stale in BOTH directions at once -- it credits the node with less than it ships on some sub-properties and with more than it ships on another -- and the document records neither difference. Distinct from :graded-satisfied-on-an-unmet-criterion (slice 4), where the criterion's own clauses simply did not hold; here the clauses describe a version of the node that has been superseded in the tree. Added by slice 5; the vocabulary is extended rather than a row rewritten."
  :node-described-only-by-drifted-citations "The node has NO row in the equations registry, so its whole account of itself is a placement argument and a delivery receipt. The implementation meets everything those two documents claim about its behaviour -- and the pointers with which they make those claims no longer resolve to the code they name, so the only writing that says what is inside the node can no longer be checked against the code by following its own citations. Distinct from :pointer-drift, which is one registry field pointing at a moved line: here the drift is the node's entire evidentiary base, and there is no registry row behind it to fall back on. Added by slice 6; the vocabulary is extended rather than a row rewritten."}

